    "youtube_description": "How was Bangladesh Bank hacked? This is the true story of the 2016 cyber heist where hackers tried to steal almost $1 billion from the New York Fed.\nA fake job email, a broken printer and a long holiday weekend. The Bangladesh Bank heist, explained in plain English.\n\nIn February 2016, hackers used Bangladesh Bank's own SWIFT system to send 35 fake payment orders to the Federal Reserve Bank of New York. They asked for about $951 million. A street name that matched a US blacklist and a simple spelling mistake stopped most of it. But $81 million still reached bank accounts in the Philippines and disappeared into casinos.\n\nTwo years later, the US Justice Department charged a North Korean programmer, Park Jin Hyok. Prosecutors say he was part of the government-backed hacking team known as Lazarus, the same group they blame for the Sony Pictures hack.\n\n{{CHAPTERS}}\n\nSources:\nhttps://www.justice.gov/archives/opa/pr/north-korean-regime-backed-programmer-charged-conspiracy-conduct-multiple-cyber-attacks-and\nhttps://www.businessinsurance.com/new-york-fed-bangladesh-bank-cyber-security-heist-fireeye-jupiter/\nhttps://amp.scmp.com/news/asia/south-asia/article/1926264/faulty-printer-and-software-led-theft-us81-million-bangladesh\nhttps://www.occrp.org/en/news/bangladesh-typo-triggers-alarm-prevents-billion-dollar-bank-heist\nhttps://www.fa-mag.com/news/printer-error-triggered-race-to-halt--1b-cyber-heist-25743.html?section=75\n\nEducational documentary based on public records. No hacking instructions.\n\n#cybersecurity #truecrime #BangladeshBank",
